# ANV-0067 -- a node durably voted twice in one term under rolling upgrade
# n2 durably voted for n2 and then for n4, both in term 4; reproduces at the
# same tick (27714) and sim-time (17386ms) across repeated invocations.
# This seed is the pinned first hit; it is not an isolated case -- the full
# 200-node-hour run (1,830 seeds) failed 267 of them (14.6%) across nearly
# every INV-RAFT-* invariant, and quadrupling the recovery time between
# transitions did not change the rate (15% at 4x vs 14.6% at 1x).
seed        = 24
workload    = raft_kv, driven by test/mixed_version_faults.cc's rolling
              upgrade/rollback schedule (RaftKvConfig::node_raft_options)
nodes       = 5
faults      = network/disk/clock drawn from the seed; random process
              crash/pause disabled; the deliberate crash-for-upgrade schedule
              (every node, twice, over ~25s, 80ms restart delay) stays on --
              it is the thing under test
invariant   = INV-RAFT-07 (a node never grants two votes in one term)
reproduce   = anvil_mixed_version_faults 24  # reproduced pre-fix; now clean
minimised   = anvil_mixed_version_faults --minimise 24 -> {partition,
              disk.bit_rot, clock.jump}, 11->3 features, 184 predicate runs,
              1-minimal verified
note        = PARTIALLY FIXED, uncommitted in-session (anvil/core/lsm/wal.h,
              anvil/core/lsm/wal.cc, anvil/core/raft/storage.cc,
              workloads/raft_kv.cc). This seed's specific mechanism -- an
              interior bit-rot hit on a HardState record with more, later,
              already-fsynced data following it in the file -- is fixed and
              verified: seed 24 no longer reproduces INV-RAFT-07 on the
              patched binary. A second sub-mechanism (bit rot landing on the
              true *last* record, indistinguishable from a torn write by file
              content alone) is not fixed; see test/corpus/ANV-0067-19.seed
              for its pinned reproduction, and BUGS.md ANV-0067 parts 2-3 for
              the full argument, including a 300-seed before/after
              measurement showing the fix's net effect on this workload is
              currently negative in raw failure count even though it is a
              verified, real partial fix -- schedule perturbation, not a new
              defect (see BUGS.md for why).
